WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.440
Film Courage: How often would you say a screenwriter’s
first draft is their final draft?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.220
John Truby, Author and Instructor: Never.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:16.130
But it brings up I think an important point
which is that the tendency for many writers

00:00:16.130 --> 00:00:24.670
is to think that because writing is rewriting
(the classic phrase that everybody says),

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:32.910
they take the approach that go ahead and write
the first draft, make mistakes, feel free

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:37.640
to make mistakes, because you can fix it later.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:41.030
This is a very dangerous approach.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:48.600
And one that seems to be a good idea at the
time but is actually the makings of a disaster.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:56.190
And the reason for that is that it means the
writer is not doing their structural work

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.650
up front, their preparation.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:08.380
In any craft before you actually execute that
art form, you need to do the prep work up

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:09.380
front.

00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.980
In story, it is figuring out the structure.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:18.159
This is why when I was talking about on a
television staff you don’t get to write

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:25.990
the first draft of the script until every
story beat is figured out in minute detail

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:28.590
and the show runner signs off on that.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:37.500
That’s because they know on television from
experience working under tremendous time pressure

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:43.840
that a lot of times (most of the time) it
doesn’t get fixed in rewrite.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:50.560
Whatever big structural mistakes you make
in the opening draft are then much more difficult

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.170
to fix.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.850
I use the metaphor of concrete.

00:01:54.850 --> 00:02:05.360
When you write that first draft of the script,
that script, that story, hardens into concrete

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.130
in your mind.

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:14.260
And now it’s more difficult to go back into
it, break up that concrete and make the kind

00:02:14.260 --> 00:02:18.740
of major structural fixes that will give you
a better script.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.840
That’s the kind of work you want to do before
you write it as a script.

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:30.290
And that’s why I put so much emphasis on
doing your character work, doing your plot

00:02:30.290 --> 00:02:35.950
work (especially emphasizing the 22 building
blocks because that gives you that sequence

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.709
of your story before you write it into a script.

00:02:39.709 --> 00:02:41.440
And one last point about this.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:49.990
When I do story consulting, I much prefer
someone not send me the screenplay, that they

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:53.170
send me the scene list.

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.970
The sequence essentially of the 3 by 5 [index]
cards.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:04.629
Because I can much more quickly figure out
what’s wrong with this story when it’s

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:10.459
simply in the scene list than I can when it’s
embedded in that concrete in the actual script

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:15.989
and there is all of this undergrowth (this
brush) that is hiding what’s really wrong

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:17.390
in all of this dialogue.

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:18.980
I don’t need that dialogue.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:20.910
I just need to know the story list.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.520
Everything comes from making that story work.

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:26.680
And that you’ve got to figure out upfront
before it goes to script.

